For a generic sample size \(N\in\{n,m\}\), let \(h_{a,P,N}\) and \(b_{a,P,N}=\theta_a/h_{a,P,N}\) denote the population Hall scale and population clip.  Define, for every \(\tau\in\mathcal T\),
\[
 \mathsf S_{a,P,N}(\tau)=
 \begin{cases}
 \displaystyle
 \frac1{f_{a,P}(\tau)h_{a,P,N}}
 \sum_{i=1}^N\left[
 \frac{\mathbf1\{A_i=a\}\psi_{a,\tau}(Y_i)}
      {\pi_{a,P}(X_i)\vee b_{a,P,N}}
 -E_P\!\left\{
 \frac{\mathbf1\{A=a\}\psi_{a,\tau}(Y)}
      {\pi_{a,P}(X)\vee b_{a,P,N}}
 \right\}\right],&a\in\mathcal A_{\mathrm H}(P),\\
 \displaystyle
 \frac1{\sqrt N}\sum_{i=1}^N\mathrm{IF}_{a,P}(O_i;\tau),&a\in\mathcal A_{\mathrm G}(P).
 \end{cases}
\]
Put \(\mathsf S_{P,N}=(\mathsf S_{0,P,N},\mathsf S_{1,P,N})\).  This definition names only an oracle comparison statistic; equality or approximation to a feasible estimator is a theorem-side obligation.
